# 2. Product positioning

This is **not**:

* another generic digital-wallet UI;
* a bKash/Nagad clone;
* a real MFS;
* a payment gateway;
* a bank;
* a blockchain system.

It is:

> **A closed-loop wallet designed around financial correctness and demonstrable reliability.**

This distinction matters because a service such as bKash operates at enormous real-world scale—its official site reports more than 83 million customers, 350,000 agents and 900,000 merchants—but its internal transaction database and locking design are not publicly documented in enough detail for us to claim we are reproducing its architecture. ([bKash][1])

We instead borrow **observable trust behaviors** and **industry-standard payment engineering patterns**.

---

# 3. Research-derived product lessons

| Source / system             | Useful lesson for us                                                                             |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| bKash                       | Transaction ID, receipts, clear send-money flows, multiple access channels, user-visible history |
| Nagad                       | Explicit transaction limits and policy constraints                                               |
| M-PESA                      | Resilience and degraded/offline user experiences matter at national scale                        |
| Stripe / PayPal             | Idempotency is mandatory for safely retrying uncertain financial requests                        |
| PostgreSQL                  | Row locking and deterministic lock ordering can protect concurrent balance changes               |
| Mojaloop / ledger platforms | Financial records should be auditable and immutable                                              |
| k6                          | Concurrency and load claims should be tested, not merely described                               |
| Toxiproxy                   | Failure behavior can be deliberately simulated locally                                           |

bKash exposes a system-generated unique transaction ID and provides transaction receipts in its app, which reinforces our decision to give every completed transfer a permanent reference and detailed receipt. ([bKash][2])

bKash also now exposes Request Money functionality, validating that requesting and accepting money is a natural complement to direct person-to-person transfer. ([bKash][3])

Nagad publicly structures limits in terms such as per-transaction, daily and monthly amount/count limits; we should adopt the **policy concept**, though our hackathon limits remain simulated product rules rather than regulatory MFS limits. ([Nagad][4])

